.TH SHIT 1 "2026-07-28" "shit" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
shit \- a fast POSIX- and Bash-compatible shell
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B shit
.RI [ options ]
.RI [ script
.RI [ argument ...]]
.br
.B shit
.B \-c
.I command
.RI [ argument ...]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B shit
is a fast POSIX- and Bash-compatible shell. An interactive prompt is run when
standard input is a terminal and no script is given. A script file named on the
command line is run, or a command string is run when it is given with
.BR \-c .
.PP
A command path ending in a directory separator fails with status 126 when its
target is not a directory. An unavailable path is reported through its first
unavailable component, and the caret covers that component.
.PP
Shell source accepts LF and CRLF line endings. A CRLF pair is read as one
newline. A lone carriage return remains part of the source text.
.PP
The shell grammar is the same on every operating system. An unquoted backslash
is an escape on Windows. A native Windows path therefore uses a quoted
backslash, an escaped backslash, or the portable forward slash. After quote
removal, Windows path operations accept both slash forms and recognize drive
and UNC roots. POSIX path operations accept only the forward slash.
.PP
The shell requires no configuration. The Linux release is static and includes
the shitbox utilities.
.SH MOODS
The shell supports four moods. The default
.B shit
mood enables strict checks and analysis by default. The
.B bash
mood accepts bash extensions with analysis disabled by default. The
.B sh
mood follows dash behavior. The
.B bash-posix
mood provides bash behavior with the POSIX option enabled and reads the bash
startup files.
.PP
On Windows,
.B echo
in the default mood leaves backslash escapes literal. The
.B \-e
option enables them. The
.B sh
mood retains dash-style default escape handling.
.PP
A binary named or symlinked to
.BR sh ", " dash ", or " bash
is matched to the corresponding mood, and diagnostics are turned off. The mood
is changed at runtime by the
.B set \-\-mood
builtin. A mood selected in a startup file remains active for the session. An
explicit
.BR "set \-u" ", " "set \-o pipefail" ", or " "set \-o failglob"
remains enabled after a mood change.
.BR "set \-o posix"
selects the
.B bash-posix
mood, and
.B "set +o posix"
selects the
.B bash
mood when the current mood is
.B bash-posix
or
.BR sh .
The option has no effect in another mood.
.SH OPTIONS
.SS Mood and startup
.TP
.BR \-M ", " \-\-mood =\fINAME\fR
NAME selects the runtime mood and accepts
.BR shit ", " bash ", " sh ", or " bash-posix .
.TP
.BR \-L ", " \-\-init\-moods =\fILIST\fR
The startup files for each listed mood are sourced in order. The list accepts
comma-separated names or repeated flags. The
.B shit
mood reads /etc/shitrc and ~/.shitrc, the
.B bash
and
.B bash-posix
moods read the bash rc and its completion, and the
.B sh
mood reads the file named by ENV. The list defaults to the value of
.BR \-\-mood .
.TP
.BR \-I ", " \-\-mimicry
A script with a recognized shell shebang runs under the matching mood.
The current diagnostics setting is preserved.
.TP
.B \-\-dumb
The flags
.BR "\-\-mood sh \-T \-\-no\-diagnostics"
are applied together.
.TP
.BR \-p ", " \-\-privileged
BASH_ENV is not sourced. Interactive rc files and login profiles are still
sourced when the real and effective ids are equal. Every startup file is
skipped in the setuid or setgid case. Without this flag, elevated ids are
dropped before execution. The privileged state is exposed through
.BR $\- ,
.BR SHELLOPTS ,
and
.BR "set \-o privileged" .
.TP
.B \-\-clean
A clean shell is started. No startup file is read in any mood, and PATH is set
to a minimal default.
.TP
.BR \-\-init\-file " " \fIFILE\fR ", " \-\-rcfile " " \fIFILE\fR
FILE is sourced as the interactive rc. The two spellings are aliases, and the
last occurrence selects the file. The shit mood sources it in place of
/etc/shitrc and ~/.shitrc. A non-login bash mood sources it in place of
~/.bashrc. A leading tilde is expanded.
.TP
.B \-\-norc
The interactive bash rc and a file named by
.B \-\-rcfile
or
.B \-\-init\-file
are skipped. Login profiles and noninteractive BASH_ENV startup are unchanged.
.TP
.BR \-r ", " \-\-restricted
Restricted mode is requested. Startup files run before its restrictions become
active. A basename of
.B rbash
requests the same mode and selects the bash mood.
.TP
.B \-\-posix
The bash-posix mood is selected, as with
.BR "\-\-mood bash-posix" .
.SS Restricted shell
A restricted shell rejects directory changes and changes to SHELL, PATH,
HISTFILE, ENV, and BASH_ENV. A command name may not contain a directory
separator. A source path, a history path, and a hash \-p path may not contain a
directory separator.
.PP
The output redirections >, >|, <>, >>, &>, &>>, and output redirection through
>& are rejected. Numeric descriptor duplication remains available. The exec
builtin, command \-p, and enable \-f or \-d are rejected. Restricted mode
cannot be disabled with set +r. Changes to the restricted_shell shopt value
are accepted without effect.
.PP
The restrictions become active after startup files finish. A sourced script
keeps them. A shell script found through PATH and executed as a command runs
without them, matching bash.
.SS Diagnostics
.TP
.B \-W[W...]
The analysis stage runs. A single
.B \-W
demotes a lenient analysis error to a warning, and the run proceeds. An
unresolved command and a variable read before it is assigned are the lenient
errors. A strict analysis error, such as a malformed glob pattern, stays fatal
under a single
.BR \-W .
A repeated
.BR \-W ,
written
.BR \-WW ,
demotes every analysis error, including strict errors, to a warning. The run
proceeds in every case. Runtime warnings are reported in the strict default mood
under
.B \-W
and in every mood under
.BR \-WW .
The set builtin names level one
.B force-warnings
and level two
.BR force-diagnostics .
Without the flag the strict default mood reports an analysis error as fatal and
stops the run before any command executes.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-diagnostics
The analysis stage is skipped. No warning or pre-run diagnostic is reported.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-init\-diagnostics
Diagnostics and warnings are suppressed while startup files are sourced. They
are restored before the prompt is displayed.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-traces
Source backtraces are suppressed for errors, warnings, and diagnostics. The
primary message, source caret, note, and detail location remain visible.
.TP
.B \-\-list\-diagnostics
The ShellCheck-style checks reported by the analysis stage are listed, then the
shell exits.
.SS Interactive
.TP
.BR \-T ", " \-\-no\-completion
Interactive tab completion and ghost-text are disabled.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-syntax\-highlighting
The syntax coloring and the ghost suggestion are disabled, and tab completion
still works.
.SS Shitbox
.TP
.B \-\-enable\-shitbox
The bundled shitbox utility names such as
.BR ls ", " mkdir ", and " cp
resolve directly as commands, the same as
.BR "set \-o shitbox" .
.SS POSIX
.TP
.BR \-c " \fICOMMAND\fR"
Commands are read from the string COMMAND.
.TP
.BR \-i ", " \-\-interactive
Interactive mode is forced.
.TP
.BR \-s ", " \-\-stdin
Commands are read from standard input.
.TP
.BR \-e ", " \-\-error\-exit
The shell exits on the first failing command.
The exemption for a non-final operand of
.B &&
or
.BR || ,
and for a command negated by
.BR ! ,
is inherited by a called function body. An otherwise unguarded function call
and an otherwise unguarded final operand keep error exit enabled.
.TP
.BR \-f ", " \-\-no\-glob
Pathname expansion is disabled.
.TP
.BR \-u ", " \-\-no\-unset
An unset variable is treated as an error.
.TP
.BR \-C ", " \-\-no\-clobber
An existing file is not overwritten through a plain redirection.
.TP
.BR \-n ", " \-\-no\-exec
The script is parsed without being run.
.TP
.BR \-t ", " \-\-one\-command
A single command is read and run, then the shell exits.
.TP
.BR \-x ", " \-\-xtrace
Each command is printed after expansion.
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\-verbose
Each line is printed as it is read.
.TP
.BR \-a ", " \-\-export\-all
Every later assignment is marked for the environment.
.TP
.BR \-l ", " \-\-login
The shell runs as a login shell.
.SS Information
.TP
.B \-\-help
The help message is displayed, then the shell exits.
.TP
.B \-\-version
The version and notices are displayed, then the shell exits.
.TP
.BR \-V ", " \-\-short\-version
The short version is displayed, then the shell exits.
.SS Output
.TP
.BR \-E ", " \-\-show\-exit\-code
The exit code is printed after each command.
.SH BASH SYNTAX
The bash language beyond POSIX sh is accepted in every mood except sh. The forms
below describe the supported bash extensions and their edge cases.
.SS Arrays
.TP
.B Indexed
An array is built with a=(x y z), an element is set with a[2]=z, and more are
appended with a+=(w). ${a[i]} reads an element through an arithmetic subscript,
${a[@]} and ${a[*]} list the elements, ${#a[@]} counts them, and ${!a[@]} lists
the indices. A negative index counts from the end. A gap left by a[5]=q makes a
sparse array whose ${!a[@]} skips the holes.
.TP
.B Associative
An associative array is declared with declare \-A m, then set with m[key]=v and
read with ${m[key]}. ${!m[@]} lists the keys, and unset 'm[key]' removes one
entry.
.TP
.B "Array quoting"
"${a[@]}" keeps each element a separate field, while "${a[*]}" joins them on the
first byte of IFS. An inner ${a[*]} inside ${x[@]+"${a[*]}"} still joins on IFS.
A read of ${a[@]} under set \-u raises an error when a is unset.
.SS Conditionals and arithmetic
.TP
.B "[[ ... ]]"
The test command takes ==, !=, the numeric \-eq family, the file tests including
\-nt and \-ot, && and ||, and =~ for a POSIX regex that fills BASH_REMATCH. The
right side of == is a glob when it is bare and a literal when it is quoted, and
=~ splits the same way. && and || short\-circuit. A malformed regex in an
unselected branch does not report an error.
.TP
.B "(( ... )) and $(( ... ))"
Arithmetic takes the C operators, ++ and \-\-, **, the ternary, the comma, and
the assignment operators. It accepts hexadecimal literals prefixed by 0x,
octal literals prefixed by 0, binary literals prefixed by 0b, and based literals
such as 16#ff. The 0b binary prefix is an extension over bash. Bash writes the
same value as 2#. A name is re\-evaluated as an expression, and a[b[c]] resolves
c, then b, then a. An empty (( )) exits 1, and an empty $(( )) expands to zero.
.SS Parameter expansion
.TP
.B Substring
${v:offset:length} cuts a substring. A negative offset is written with a leading
space, ${v: \-3}. Without the space ${v:\-3} is the default\-value form. A
negative length stops that many characters from the end.
.TP
.B Replacement
${v/pat/rep} replaces the first match and ${v//pat/rep} every match, anchored by
/# at the front and /% at the end. An & in the replacement stands for the
matched text, and \\& is a literal &.
.TP
.B "Case and transform"
${v^^} and ${v,,} change the case of every character, and ${v^} and ${v,} change
the first, each with an optional pattern. ${v@Q} quotes the value, ${v@E}
expands backslash escapes, ${v@A} prints an assignment, ${v@P} expands a prompt
template, and ${v@a} reports attributes. The ${v@U}, ${v@L}, and ${v@u}
transforms apply the corresponding case changes.
.TP
.B Indirection
${!ref} reads the variable that ref names. ${!pre*} and ${!pre@} list the names
that start with pre.
.SS Word generation
.TP
.B "Brace expansion"
{a,b,c} lists words, {1..10} and {a..z} count a range, {1..10..2} adds a step,
and {001..10} pads the width. Braces nest and multiply. A lone {a} stays
literal.
.TP
.B Quoting
$'...' reads the C escapes \\n, \\t, \\xHH, and \\OOO. $"..." marks a string
for translation. It behaves like an ordinary double-quoted string when no
catalog is present.
.TP
.B "Redirection and substitution"
&> and &>> send both output streams to a file, and |& pipes both. <(cmd) and
>(cmd) are process substitutions on a /dev/fd path, and <<< is a here\-string
that appends one newline. A {var}> or {var}< redirection allocates a free
descriptor numbered ten or above, performs the redirection on it, and binds the
number to the variable. The {var}>&\- form closes the descriptor named by the
variable.
.TP
.B Globbing
shopt \-s extglob turns on ?(), *(), +(), @(), and !(), and shopt \-s globstar
turns on **. dotglob, nullglob, and failglob change how a leading dot, a
non\-match, and a failed match are treated. In the default mood a non\-matching
glob and a glob in command position are fatal, and a compatibility mood warns.
.SS Builtins
.TP
.B Attributes
declare, local, typeset, and readonly take \-a, \-A, \-i, \-r, and \-x. printf
takes \-v to assign its result, and mapfile and readarray with \-t read the
lines of the input into an array.
.TP
.B "Pipeline status"
Under set \-o pipefail the pipeline reports the rightmost failing stage, and the
default mood turns pipefail on. PIPESTATUS holds every stage's status in either
case.
.TP
.B "Word splitting"
An unquoted $@ or $* splits on IFS. "$@" keeps the parameters apart, and "$*"
joins them on the first byte of IFS. A run of a non\-whitespace IFS character
makes an empty field, and a run of whitespace collapses.
.TP
.B read
read \-r keeps a backslash literal. A plain read treats a trailing backslash as
a line continuation.
.SH LINE EDITING
The interactive line editor starts in emacs mode. A vi-style modal editor is
selected by
.B set \-o vi
and the emacs default is restored by
.BR "set \-o emacs" .
Ghost text uses prefix matches from completion and history. Tab also offers
smart-case and subsequence matches.
An empty command position includes builtins, functions, aliases, shitbox
utilities, and programs from PATH.
Quoted, escaped, variable, and tilde directory prefixes remain in their typed
form when a later unquoted slash or basename is completed.
.PP
Syntax highlighting distinguishes shell syntax, commands, flags, variables,
paths, and globs. Invalid input is marked in the source line.
.PP
In vi mode each line begins in insert mode. Escape enters command mode. Command
mode provides the motions, the operators with counts, the named registers, the
change repeat, and a visual selection. The visual selection is charwise from
.B v
and whole lines from
.BR V .
The last edit is undone by
.B u
in command mode and by
.B Ctrl\-_
in either mode. The undone edit is redone by
.BR Ctrl\-^ ,
or by
.B Ctrl\-R
in command mode. The incremental history search is opened by
.B Ctrl\-R
in insert mode and by the
.B /
key in command mode. Enter accepts the highlighted match into the line and
leaves the search without running it. Ctrl\-G cancels the search, and the line
is restored to its state from before the search began. The colon key opens an
ex command. The commands
.BR q ", " q! ", " wq ", " wq! ", " x ", and " quit
exit the shell. The cursor shape tracks the submode, a bar in insert, a block in
command, and an underline in visual or while an operator waits for its motion.
In command mode the arrow keys step between the buffer rows and do not recall
history. The insert-mode arrows still recall it. With an operator pending, Up or
Down acts on the current line together with the line above or below. The dk and
dj commands perform the corresponding linewise operations.
.PP
A block selection is entered with
.B Ctrl\-V
in command mode. It creates a rectangular selection across several lines. The
keys
.BR d " and " x
delete the rectangle, and
.BR I ", " c ", or " C
insert the typed text at the left column of every selected line. Several lines
are indented at once. The arrows and
.B h j k l
move within the rectangle.
.PP
The
.B Ctrl\-V
key enters multi-cursor mode in emacs mode without selecting text. A cursor sits
at the same column on every spanned line, and
.BR Up " or " Down
grows the span by one line. A typed character is inserted at every cursor at
once, and Backspace erases at every cursor. The edit is left with
.BR Ctrl\-G .
.SH RESCUE
A login shell enters rescue mode when SHIT_FLAGS or a command-line flag is
invalid and standard input is a terminal.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B SHIT_FLAGS
The default flags are applied before the command line. A flag on the command
line still wins.
.TP
.B SHIT_HISTORY
The command history is stored in the named file, ~/.shit_history by default.
The history builtin reads, writes, and clears it through the \fB\-r\fP,
\fB\-w\fP, and \fB\-c\fP flags.
.TP
.B SHIT_DIRECTORY_HISTORY
The z builtin reads its frecency store from the named file,
~/.shit_directory_history by default.
.TP
.B SHIT_CALC_HISTORY
Interactive calc history is stored in the named file, ~/.shit_calc_history by
default.
.TP
.B ENV
The sh mood sources the file named by ENV.
.TP
.B BASH_ENV
A noninteractive bash mood sources this file before its command, standard input,
or script runs. Parameter expansion, command substitution, arithmetic
expansion, and a leading tilde are applied to its value. PATH is not searched.
Interactive, bash-posix, clean, privileged, and rescue sessions do not source
it.
.TP
.B PATH
Commands are searched through the colon\-separated directories in PATH. The
\-\-clean flag resets it to a minimal default.
.TP
.B HOME
Tilde expansion and the default startup and history paths use HOME as the home
directory.
.TP
.B PWD
The cd and z builtins maintain the current working directory in PWD.
.TP
.B OLDPWD
The cd and z builtins store the previous working directory in OLDPWD. The
command cd \- returns to it.
.TP
.B SHELL
The environment supplies the shell path through SHELL. The running binary path
is used when the variable was initially unset.
.TP
.B SHLVL
The shell nesting depth is inherited through SHLVL and incremented by a child
shell.
.TP
.B CDPATH
The cd builtin searches the colon\-separated directories in CDPATH for a
relative operand. When an explicit directory is missing, cd reports its first
unavailable component. A close sibling directory is suggested when one exists.
.TP
.B IFS
Word splitting and the join of $* use the separators in IFS. The default value
contains a space, a tab, and a newline.
.TP
.B "COLUMNS, LINES"
An interactive shell stores the terminal width and height in COLUMNS and LINES.
.TP
.B PROMPT_COMMAND
The command in PROMPT_COMMAND runs before each interactive prompt is drawn.
.TP
.B TERM
Color and interactive decorations on POSIX systems use the terminal type named
by TERM.
.TP
.B MANPATH
Completion searches the manual paths in MANPATH for subcommands and options.
.TP
.B PIPESTATUS
The exit status of each stage of the last pipeline is stored in PIPESTATUS.
.TP
.B REPLY
The read builtin stores a line in REPLY when no name is given. The select loop
also stores its input there.
.TP
.B "OPTIND, OPTARG"
The getopts builtin maintains its option index and argument in OPTIND and
OPTARG.
.TP
.B TIMEFORMAT
The time builtin formats its report with TIMEFORMAT.
.TP
.B PS1
The primary prompt is rendered from PS1. A default template is supplied when it
is unset.
.TP
.B PS0
The prompt in PS0 is printed after a command line is read and before it runs.
Its default value is empty.
.TP
.B PS2
The continuation prompt is read from PS2 and defaults to "> ".
.TP
.B PS3
The select loop reads its prompt from PS3, which defaults to "#? ".
.TP
.B PS4
The set \-x trace uses PS4 as its prefix. The value defaults to "+ ", and its
first character is repeated once per enclosing subshell.
.TP
.B BASH_XTRACEFD
The set \-x trace is written to the descriptor in BASH_XTRACEFD. Standard error
is used when the variable is unset or invalid.
.TP
.B SHIT
The path used to invoke the shell is available as SHIT in every mood.
.TP
.B SHIT_VERSION
The shell version is available in SHIT_VERSION in a form such as 0.0.8\-beta.
.TP
.B SHIT_COMMIT
The commit used to build the binary is available in SHIT_COMMIT. Its value is
<no git> when no commit was recorded.
.TP
.B SHIT_BUILD_MODE
The binary build mode is available in SHIT_BUILD_MODE, such as dbg, rel, or cov.
.TP
.B SHIT_OS
The distribution and kernel identification of the build host are available in
SHIT_OS.
.TP
.B SHIT_GIT_BRANCH
The repository containing the working directory supplies SHIT_GIT_BRANCH. A
detached head supplies a short commit hash, and a directory outside a repository
supplies an empty value. An assignment overrides the detected value.
.TP
.B SHIT_IDENTITY
The lowercase SHA-256 identity of the running executable is exported through
SHIT_IDENTITY. The value is read-only, and an inherited value is discarded.
.TP
.B NO_COLOR
Color is turned off in the prompt, the diagnostics, and shitbox cat syntax
highlighting when the variable is set to a nonempty value.
.TP
.B SHIT_ANSI_*
A color escape is provided under each of these names. The recognized colors are
BLACK, RED, GREEN, YELLOW, BLUE, MAGENTA, CYAN, and WHITE. Each color also has a
BRIGHT_ prefixed high\-intensity form. BOLD, DIM, and RESET are also provided.
The names resolve in every mood. A recognized name reads back empty when color
is off. A nonempty NO_COLOR value and a redirected prompt turn the escapes off,
and the prompt stays plain. A stored assignment to one of the names wins over
the provided escape.
.TP
.B "RANDOM, SRANDOM, SECONDS, LINENO, EPOCHSECONDS, EPOCHREALTIME"
These dynamic values resolve on each read. RANDOM and SRANDOM give a fresh
random value,
SECONDS counts the seconds since the shell started, LINENO gives the line of the
current command, and EPOCHSECONDS and EPOCHREALTIME give the wall clock time in
seconds. They resolve in every mood but sh.
.TP
.B "BASHPID, PPID, UID, EUID, GROUPS, HOSTNAME, HOSTTYPE, MACHTYPE, OSTYPE"
These process and host values resolve on each read. BASHPID is the process id,
PPID is the parent, UID and EUID are the real and the effective user, GROUPS is
the real group, and HOSTNAME, HOSTTYPE, MACHTYPE, and OSTYPE name the host and
the platform. They resolve in every mood but sh.
.TP
.B "SHELLOPTS, BASH_SUBSHELL, BASH_ARGV0, BASH_EXECUTION_STRING, BASH_MONOSECONDS"
These shell state values resolve on each read. SHELLOPTS is a colon-separated
list of the enabled set \-o options and reflects later set changes.
BASH_SUBSHELL counts the subshell depth, BASH_ARGV0 is the shell name,
BASH_EXECUTION_STRING is the argument of \-c, and BASH_MONOSECONDS is a
monotonic millisecond counter. They resolve in every mood but sh.
.TP
.B "FUNCNAME, BASH_SOURCE, BASH_LINENO, BASH_COMMAND"
These call stack values describe the running command. FUNCNAME names the
function in progress, BASH_SOURCE is the file it was read from, BASH_LINENO is
the line of the call, and BASH_COMMAND is the command being executed. The array
forms give the whole call stack. They resolve in every mood but sh.
.TP
.B BASH_REMATCH
BASH_REMATCH contains the groups captured by the last [[ ... =~ ... ]] match.
.TP
.B "BASH, BASH_VERSION, BASH_VERSINFO"
A bash identity is advertised under these names so a bash rc detects it. They
are seeded only in the bash mood. BASH is the path to the shell, BASH_VERSION is
the version string, and BASH_VERSINFO is its indexed components.
.TP
.B "COMP_WORDS, COMP_CWORD, COMP_LINE, COMP_POINT, COMP_WORDBREAKS, COMPREPLY"
A programmable completion function reads these and writes COMPREPLY. COMP_WORDS
holds the words on the line, COMP_CWORD is the index of the word under the
cursor, COMP_LINE is the whole line, COMP_POINT is the cursor offset, and
COMP_WORDBREAKS is the characters that split a word.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/shitrc
/etc/shitrc is the system startup file for the shit mood.
.TP
.I ~/.shitrc
~/.shitrc is the user startup file for the shit mood.
.TP
.I ~/.shit_history
~/.shit_history is the default command history file.
.TP
.I ~/.shit_directory_history
~/.shit_directory_history is the default frecency store for the z builtin.
.TP
.I ~/.shit_calc_history
~/.shit_calc_history is the default history file for interactive calc.
.SH SHITBOX
The
.B shitbox
builtin provides a set of small bundled core utilities.
.B "shitbox ls"
runs the bundled ls. An unknown utility name reports an error. With the
.B \-\-enable\-shitbox
flag or
.B "set \-o shitbox"
the utility names resolve directly as commands without the prefix. A utility
is used when PATH holds no program of that name. A binary whose name matches a
utility acts as that utility. Every utility supports \-\-help. A utility invoked
through a matching binary name supports \-\-version.
.PP
.BI "shitbox \-\-assimilate " DIR
installs one such symlink per utility into DIR. In the default shit mood a bare
utility name also falls back to the bundled utility when PATH holds no binary of
that name. A PATH binary takes precedence in every mood.
.TP
.B basename
The final path component is printed, with support for an optional suffix
operand.
.TP
.B calc
Arithmetic arguments are evaluated, or expressions are read with \-i or \-p.
.TP
.B cat
Files or standard input are concatenated, and \-n enables line numbering.
The
.B \-\-syntax\-highlighting
flag colors shell source when standard output is a terminal. A shell extension
or a known shell shebang selects the source. A file that contains a null byte
is written without highlighting.
.TP
.B cp
Files or directory trees are copied with support for \-r, \-R, and \-v.
.TP
.B dirname
The directory portion of one path is printed.
.TP
.B du
Path sizes are totaled without following symlinks. The \-s and \-h flags are
supported.
.TP
.B env
Temporary assignments can be applied before a command, or the environment can
be printed.
.TP
.B find
Sorted trees are walked with support for \-name, \-type, \-maxdepth,
\-mindepth, and \-print.
.TP
.B flock
A command is run while a directory lock is held.
.TP
.B grep
Regular-expression matches are printed with support for \-i and \-v.
.TP
.B head
The first ten lines are printed by default. The \-n and \-c flags select a
count.
.TP
.B killall
Processes are signaled by exact name. Signal selection and listing use \-s,
\-\-signal, \-l, and \-\-list.
.TP
.B ln
Symbolic links are created with support for \-s and \-f.
.TP
.B ls
Sorted entries are listed with support for \-a, \-A, \-1, \-l, and \-h.
.TP
.B make
Compact make rules are run with support for \-f, \-\-file, \-C, and
\-\-directory. The unused \-B and \-k flags are accepted, and \-j is ignored.
.TP
.B mkdir
Directories are created with support for \-p and an octal \-m mode.
.TP
.B mv
Sources are renamed with support for \-f and \-v.
.TP
.B nproc
The available logical processor count is printed. The \-\-all and
\-\-ignore=COUNT options are supported.
.TP
.B pkill
Processes whose names match a pattern are signaled. Signal selection and listing
use \-s, \-\-signal, \-l, and \-\-list.
.TP
.B ps
Processes are listed with support for \-a, \-u, \-x, and the accepted \-w flag.
.TP
.B realpath
The canonical absolute form of each path is printed.
.TP
.B rm
Paths are removed with support for \-r, \-R, \-f, and \-\-dry\-run. Root, dot,
and dot-dot are refused.
.TP
.B rmdir
Empty directories are removed.
.TP
.B seq
Signed integer sequences are printed with a nonzero step.
.TP
.B sleep
Execution pauses for summed durations with optional s, m, h, or d suffixes.
.TP
.B sort
Input lines are sorted in byte order, and \-r reverses the result.
.TP
.B tail
The last ten lines are printed by default. The \-n and \-c flags select a
count.
.TP
.B tee
Standard input is copied to output files, and \-a enables append mode.
.TP
.B timeout
A command is supervised for a suffixed duration. Signal, kill delay, and status
behavior use \-s, \-\-signal, \-k, \-\-kill\-after, \-p, and
\-\-preserve\-status.
.TP
.B touch
File times are updated, and \-c avoids creating missing files.
.TP
.B tr
Input bytes are translated with ranges and character classes, and \-d enables
deletion.
.TP
.B uniq
Adjacent equal lines are collapsed, and \-c prints run counts.
.TP
.B unlink
One non-directory path is removed.
.TP
.B wc
Lines, words, and bytes are counted with support for \-l, \-w, and \-c.
.TP
.B which
Builtins, PATH programs, and shitbox utilities are resolved. The \-a, \-\-all,
\-q, and \-\-quiet options are supported.
.TP
.B whoami
The current user name is printed.
.TP
.B yes
The joined operands, or y, are repeated until output closes.
.SH SHIT BUILTINS
These builtins are specific to shit.
.TP
.BI "assimilate " TARGET
The running shell is copied through scp to an SSH target and installed as
.B shit
in one writable and searchable directory in the remote PATH. A directory whose
path contains sbin is skipped. /usr/local/bin and $HOME/.local/bin are preferred
when they occur in PATH. Every requested link and the binary are installed in
one directory, and a directory is skipped when an existing path would be
clobbered. The
.BI \-\-link\-mood= MOOD
option requests a relative link named bash, dash, sh, or shit. The option can be
repeated, and each value can contain comma-separated moods. The
.BR \-x " or " \-\-trace
option prints remote installation progress. The
.BI \-\-ssh\-command= COMMAND
and
.BI \-\-scp\-command= COMMAND
options replace ssh and scp. COMMAND is split into arguments at literal spaces.
The remote host needs a POSIX-compatible SSH login shell that can launch the
transferred executable. The installed executable is validated before
replacement. A validation or installation error restores the prior target, and
concurrent installations are serialized. An interrupted installation can leave
temporary files.
.TP
.B bench
bench runs each command repeatedly and prints the wall time, peak resident set,
and available performance counters as a mean, standard deviation, and range.
With more than one command, each later command is compared with the first. Each
sample runs the command through
.BR "shit \-c" .
Sampling stops when a sample returns a nonzero exit code unless
.B \-\-ignore\-exit\-code
is passed. The command output is suppressed unless
.B \-\-show\-output
is passed.
The
.B \-\-no\-shell
option runs the command directly. The
.B \-\-runs
option sets the exact sample count, and the
.B \-\-duration
option sets the sampling budget in milliseconds.
.TP
.B z
The shell changes to a frequently visited directory ranked by a frecency store
at ~/.shit_directory_history. The store weights each directory by its visit
count and how recently it was seen. The operands join into one query, and a
directory is matched by a case-insensitive substring.
.SH BASH BUILTIN EXTENSIONS
These builtins follow bash semantics.
.TP
.B set
.RI [ option ]
.RI [ argument ...]
A minus enables a shell option and a plus disables it. set \-o NAME and set +o
NAME select the same state by long name. With no name, set \-o prints the
current states, and set +o prints reusable commands. set \-\-options prints
every option with its state and description. set \-E controls errtrace, set \-P
makes cd resolve symbolic links, set \-T controls functrace, and set \-B
controls brace expansion. Runtime set \-n stops later commands in the current
command list. Runtime set \-r enables restricted mode and cannot disable it
again with set +r.
Changes made in a command substitution are restored when the substitution ends.
SHELLOPTS, $\-, [[ \-o NAME ]], and completion report the shared state.
.TP
.B enable
.RB [ \-a ]
.RB [ \-d ]
.RB [ \-n ]
.RB [ \-f
.IR file ]
.RB [ \-s ]
.RI [ name ...]
Every builtin remains enabled. The \-d, \-n, \-f, and \-s compatibility flags
are accepted without effect, and \-a lists every builtin.
.TP
.B hash
.RB [ \-r ]
.RB [ \-p
.IR path ]
.RI [ name ...]
Resolved command locations are remembered or listed. The \-r flag clears the
cache. The \-p flag records PATH as the location of each NAME.
.TP
.B bind
bind accepts its flags and operands without changing key bindings and returns
zero.
.TP
.B caller
Without an operand the builtin prints nothing and returns one at the top level
and zero inside a function or a sourced file. With an operand the operand is a
frame index, and the builtin prints the line number and the source file of that
frame. A non-numeric operand reports an error.
.TP
.B help
Without a pattern every builtin name is printed one per line. With a pattern the
pattern is a builtin name, and the builtin help is rendered the same way the
target's
.B \-\-help
flag renders it. A name that is not a builtin reports an error. The
.B \-d
short description flag and the
.B \-m
manpage flag are accepted without effect.
.TP
.B logout
logout exits a login shell after the EXIT trap runs. Its optional operand is the
exit status. In a non-login shell it reports an error and returns one. The
.B exit
builtin exits a non-login shell.
.TP
.B suspend
The shell is stopped with SIGSTOP and resumes on SIGCONT. A login shell is
refused unless
.B \-f
is passed to force the suspension.
.SH EXIT STATUS
The shell exits with the status of the last command it ran. A usage error such
as an unknown flag in a non-interactive run exits with status 2.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR bash (1),
.BR dash (1),
.BR sh (1),
.BR fish (1),
.BR busybox (1)
